WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.672
If you tell a friend there are five red cars in a row, you do not say red, red, red, red, red. You say five red. Run-length encoding uses exactly this idea to make data smaller.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:25.367
A run is a group of the same symbol, or the same pixel colour, sitting next to each other. RLE replaces each run with a count and the symbol. So four whites become four W.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:37.427
RLE works well when data has long runs, like a simple icon with big blocks of one colour. It works badly when values keep changing, and can even make the file bigger.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:49.686
Read the data left to right, one row at a time. Count how many of the same symbol appear before it changes. Write the count and the symbol, then start counting the next run.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:57.994
Finally, check each line. The counts must add up to the width of the row. If they do not, you have missed or doubled a pixel.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:14.033
Here is a real question from Paper 1 in November 2021. An icon is set out in an eight by eight grid of black and white pixels. Use run-length encoding to compress the first four lines. It is worth three marks.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:23.603
Here is the icon as a grid. Line one is white, white, white, black, then four whites. That is three W, one B, four W.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.939
Line two is one white, six black, one white. So one W, six B, one W.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:40.548
Line three changes a lot. One B, two W, one B, one W, one B, two W. Line four is three W, two B, three W.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:52.279
Here is the full answer, with every line checked against eight. All four lines correct earns three marks. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:02.211
A common mistake is counting all the whites in a line together. Line three has three separate white runs. Each run must be written on its own.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:12.886
Now you try. Encode these two rows of ten pixels with RLE. Then decide which row RLE suits, and why. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:30.448
Row A is five W, two B, three W. That is six symbols instead of ten, so it is smaller. Row B has ten runs of one, so it becomes twenty symbols. That is bigger than the original. RLE suits row A, because it has long runs.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:41.901
Remember the method: read along each row, count each run, write count and symbol, and check the counts add up to the width. Now try the practice questions for this type.
